WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:03.968
-The Russian invasion of Ukraine
has the world on edge,

00:00:03.968 --> 00:00:06.267
as all eyes are on the man
leading the country,

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:09.167
a former comedian turned
president, Volodymyr Zelenskyy.

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:10.767
Here with more
is one of my writers,

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:13.267
a native Russian
who emigrated to America

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:16.801
from the former Soviet Union,
Dina Gusovsky, everybody.

00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:19.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:20.968
-Thanks, Seth.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:23.501
That's right.
I am a Russian Jewish refugee.

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:27.167
My family and I fled the former
Soviet Union 30 years ago.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.133
In fact, here's a picture
of me and my mom

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:32.400
right before we came to America.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.667
[ Audience aws ]
Thank you.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.267
Apparently her tickets
to the United States

00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.968
were good for one woman
and one ventriloquist dummy.

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:42.834
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.100
I mean, it's scary enough
coming to a country

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.434
where I did not know
the language and culture,

00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:51.300
and it's even more terrifying
that I did it looking like this.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.701
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.501
This picture was from the 1990s,

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:57.267
but apparently the cameras
in Russia at the time

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:00.167
were from the 1920s.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:05.567
To take this picture, I had to
sit still for 30 minutes.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.634
And despite the fact that I did
grow out of being a tiny man,

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:12.734
to this day, I often feel like

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:15.100
a walking contradiction
in America.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.200
I mean, I eat Russian food,
immerse myself in the culture,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.334
and I still speak Russian
every day

00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:23.501
to my parents, my grandmother,
and people from Greenpeace

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:25.567
asking me to sign
their petitions.

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:28.200
-Oh, really?
What do you say, Dina?

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.501
-[ Speaking Russian ]

00:01:31.501 --> 00:01:33.734
-Oh, that really sounds scary.
What does it mean?

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:37.100
-It means, "I don't have time,
but have a wonderful day."

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:41.000
And look, just because
someone in America

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.367
speaks Russian
and always looks miserable,

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:45.734
that doesn't mean
that they support Putin.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:49.067
But I can see how that can be
confusing to non-Russians,

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.834
especially since the good guy
and the bad guy

00:01:51.834 --> 00:01:54.701
are both named Vladimir.

00:01:54.701 --> 00:01:57.934
And despite the fact that
I had been told many times

00:01:57.934 --> 00:01:59.501
to "go back to Russia" --

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:02.067
shout-out to
my fifth grade teacher...

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:03.834
[ Audience groans ]

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:08.501
...yeah -- I am so, so grateful
to be an American.

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:12.400
I have never been more proud of
my parents for bringing me here.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.467
And I have never felt luckier
than to work for you, Seth.

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:17.701
-Me?

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:20.400
-To have a comedian as a boss?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.133
President Zelenskyy
is showing everyone

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:25.934
just how brave comedians are.

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:27.534
Right, Seth?

00:02:27.534 --> 00:02:30.067
-Uh, uh-huh. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:33.400
-I bet if you could, you would
go to Ukraine right now

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.701
and fight alongside
the Ukrainians.

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:38.133
-I -- To-- I mean, yeah.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:39.467
Wish I could.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.434
-Oh, you can.
-Sorry, what's that?

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:42.834
-You totally can.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:45.133
Foreign nationals
are not just allowed,

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:48.501
but encouraged to go to Ukraine
to fight for freedom.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.801
I'm just [bleep] with you.

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:53.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:57.734
I didn't think you'd go.
Nobody did.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:01.634
Look, Seth, we can't forget,
this is Putin's war.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:03.367
And while he might be trying

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:06.234
to divide Russians
and Ukrainians over there,

00:03:06.234 --> 00:03:09.033
here in America,
we are united and steadfast

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:11.234
in our support for Ukraine.

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:14.501
I have so many Russian
and Ukrainian immigrant friends.

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:16.000
We speak Russian.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.501
We eat a beet soup
called borsch,

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:21.300
served with sour cream
and raw garlic.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.901
And when we really want to treat
ourselves, sunflower seeds.

00:03:26.901 --> 00:03:29.033
Another thing
we have in common --

00:03:29.033 --> 00:03:32.400
We're all very close with
our babushkas, our grandmothers.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.734
My grandmother was born
in Kirovohrad, Ukraine,

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:38.767
survived the Holocaust,
and witnessed the Cold War.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:42.000
She was only able to come
to America in her 60s,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.734
and she happens to be this
country's biggest cheerleader.

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:47.000
This is her.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.300
[ Audience aws ]

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:55.601
She -- She loves America.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:58.534
And, Seth, it's like
my babushka says.

00:03:58.534 --> 00:04:00.567
[ Speaking Russian ]

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:06.033
-Was that just another
harsh-sounding Russian thing

00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:07.334
that wasn't so bad?

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:13.367
-No, it means "[Bleep] [bleep]
[bleep] [bleep] [bleep]"

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:14.934
-Dina!

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:17.167
-Sorry.
Grandma's got a filthy mouth.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:19.801
-Alright, we'll let it go.
Dina Gusovsky, everybody.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:20.801
We'll be right back.

